INT. FUNERAL PARLOR - BACK ROOM - AFTERNOON

A narrow room behind the chapel, lined with folding chairs and a coffee urn. Sun through frosted glass makes the floor look wet. Chrysanthemums crowd a card table. Through the wall comes the muffled sound of a hymn.

LEOPOLD VANCE, sixties, in a black suit that fits him like borrowed armor, pours coffee into two paper cups with great care. His sister HESTER, early sixties, in a navy dress, sits very straight, hands folded over her purse.

Neither of them looks at the woman standing in the doorway.

A WOMAN, forties, in a plain gray coat, damp at the shoulders. She holds a small wrapped box with both hands, as if it might spill.

WOMAN
Excuse me. I'm sorry. I didn't know where to go.

LEOPOLD
The service is in the chapel.

WOMAN
I know. I stood at the back. Then I left.

HESTER
You should have stayed.

WOMAN
I didn't think I was welcome.

Leopold sets a cup in front of Hester and holds the other out toward the woman.

LEOPOLD
Coffee.

She takes it. Drinks. Her hands tremble just slightly.

WOMAN
Thank you.

HESTER
You were his friend?

WOMAN
For nineteen years.

LEOPOLD
Nineteen. He never mentioned you.

WOMAN
No. He wouldn't have.

HESTER
Why not?

The woman looks at the box. At the two of them. At the hymn leaking through the wall.

WOMAN
My name is Adaeze. Adaeze Nwosu. He used to bring me flowers on Thursdays. Chrysanthemums.

Leopold glances at the card table. At the flowers. His hand tightens around the urn spout.

LEOPOLD
Those are his favorites.

ADAEZE
I know. He told me.

HESTER
Leo.

LEOPOLD
It's all right, Hester.

HESTER
It is not all right. Who is this woman?

ADAEZE
I'm not here to take anything. I brought him something.

She places the box on the table. Hester doesn't touch it. Leopold does, slowly, and peels back the paper. Inside, a small clay bowl, hand-glazed, a crack mended in gold.

LEOPOLD
You made this.

ADAEZE
I'm a potter. He came to my studio the day his wife died. He sat for hours. I didn't know his name until the second week.

HESTER
Our brother was married to Lena for forty years.

ADAEZE
Yes. And she died in March. And he came to me in April.

Leopold lowers himself into a folding chair, the bowl in both hands.

LEOPOLD
He was lonely.

HESTER
He had us.

ADAEZE
He said that too. He said you were kind and you never asked him anything.

Hester opens her mouth to answer and doesn't. Outside, the hymn ends. A pause, then the rustle of a congregation rising.

LEOPOLD
(softly)
Will you sit with us for the burial, Adaeze?

ADAEZE
If you'll have me.

LEOPOLD
We will.

Hester stares at the bowl. At the gold line through the clay. Then she pulls out a handkerchief from her purse and, without a word, offers it to Adaeze.
